Key use cases for AI observability
Monitor service health and performance: Track real-time metrics (request counts, durations, and error rates). Stay aligned with SLOs.
Monitor service quality and cost: Implement error budgets for performance and cost control. Validate model consumption and response times. Prevent quality degradation by monitoring models and usage patterns in real time.
Evaluate response quality: Score the quality, safety, and relevance of GenAI and agent responses with online LLM-as-a-judge evaluations. Catch regressions on live production traffic and track score drift over time.
End-to-end tracing and debugging: Trace prompt flows from initial request to final response for quick root cause analysis and troubleshoot. Gain granular visibility into LLM prompt latencies and model-level metrics. Pinpoint issues in prompts, tokens, or system integrations.
Build trust, reduce compliance and audit risks: Track every input and output for an audit trail. Query all data in real time and store for future reference. Maintain full data lineage from prompt to response.
By embracing AI observability, organizations improve reliability, trustworthiness, and overall performance, leading to more robust and responsible AI deployments.

Observing Agents and Agentic workloads
Get visibility into Agentic AI workloads: agent execution paths, tool invocations, and inter-agent communication. Monitor and debug Agent interactions such as function calling, LLM calls, tool-use, RAG, and resolve performance, latency, cost, and reliability issues.

Dynatrace integrates with workloads such as OpenAI Agent SDK, LangChain/LangGraph Agents, CrewAI, Amazon Bedrock Agentcore, MCP tools, Google ADK, and many more.

Evaluating agent response quality
Run online, LLM-as-a-judge evaluations against production AI and agent traces and prompts already available in Dynatrace. Each evaluation score is written back as a business event and remains linked to its source trace, so you can investigate failures in their full runtime context.

View the results in the Evaluations tab of the AI Observability app, where you can monitor pass and fail rates, analyze individual evaluation results, and detect score drift over time.

To learn about evaluation concepts such as LLM-as-a-judge, see Terms and concepts about AI Observability and GenAI in Dynatrace. To configure and run evaluations end to end with the open-source dt-evals CLI, see Run LLM-as-a-judge evaluations with dt-evals.

dt-evals includes more than 10 built-in LLM judge evaluators for detecting issues such as:

User frustration in incoming prompts
Toxic, harmful, or unsafe responses
Irrelevant or incomplete answers
Hallucinations and unsupported claims
Poor faithfulness to retrieved context
PII leakage, prompt injection, and harmful bias
In addition to these judge evaluators, dt-evals runs statistical drift detection, comparing recent scores against a rolling baseline of earlier results to surface regressions over time.

Observing model providers and platforms
Dynatrace integrates with providers such as OpenAI, Amazon Bedrock, NVIDIA NIM, Ollama to monitor performance (token consumption, latency, availability, and errors) at scale.

Observing semantic caches and vector databases
Vector databases and semantic caches are central to RAG architectures. Dynatrace monitors solutions like Milvus, Weaviate, and Qdrant to help identify performance bottlenecks and usage anomalies.

Observing orchestration frameworks
Frameworks like LangChain manage data ingestion and prompt engineering for RAG applications. Dynatrace ensures you can track performance, versions, and degradation points in these pipelines.

Observing infrastructure and resources
Monitor infrastructure usage (GPU/TPU metrics, temperature, memory, etc.) for cloud services such as Amazon Elastic Inference and Google TPU, or custom hardware like NVIDIA GPU. This helps optimize resources and supports sustainability initiatives.

